title: Query planner
tags: [[Query engine]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: A minimal planner, oriented to the store: the scans of an AND run last on its running intersection, and a few candidates are checked on their own rows instead of walking a field.

The original indexing design had an ''adaptive planner'' choosing an access path per
operand. It was taken off the roadmap in September 2026: in the resident engine each
operand had exactly one way to be answered, so there was nothing to choose.

On the key-value store an operand can again be answered several ways — a posting or a
walk of the value partition, a per-candidate check or a scan of distinct values, a
fetch or an ordered walk — and small cost choices are already made, each by a fixed
rule on exact counts: fetch versus walk ([[Sorting and postings]]), lazy text checks
([[Text search in the store]]), `osm` anchors cut to a rarer term's folders
([[The forest in the store]]), reading versus walking by uuid order.

So a planner "may be useful again — to be seen" (user, September 2026): minimal and
oriented to the store — one cost choice at a time, from what profiling finds — not the
old design, whose SQL-specific parts are obsolete and whose ambition was probably too
great. The local choices above are not a violation of anything.

!! What the planner decides

It was built in October 2026 (`index/plan.rs`). No cost model of the whole query: each
choice is a rule on the query's shape, on an exact count the store already holds, or
(since November 2026) on a histogram's estimate.

Measured cold (November 2026), the "probe or walk" rule chose wrong by twenty, and
statistics are to replace the fixed rules: [[Planner statistics]] (proposed; probe or
walk and the order of an `and` now read them).

* ''The order of an `and`.'' Its operands answered from a bitmap or a posting — presence,
  equality, `in`, a subtree, a follow, `uuid_in` — are evaluated first, in the query's
  order; the ''scans'' last: `matches`, `osm direct`, `!=` and the ordered comparisons, each
  handed the running intersection as its candidates, the narrowest first by its estimate
  ([[Planner statistics]]). Once the intersection is empty, nothing more is evaluated.
  The order depends on the data, so a page may be served by another path than the one
  before it if the data changed between them; it gets the same ''results'', since an
  `and` commutes.
* ''Probe or walk.'' A scan given candidates checks their own rows (a uuid and the
  field's rows each, about four keys, scattered: weighed four times a walked key —
  `plan::probes`) when that costs less than the walk; otherwise it walks the field's
  values. The walk of a `!=` or a text predicate is priced at the field's holders; an
  ordered comparison of a number or a date at the rows its histogram gives the range, a
  value with a posting counted as its posting ([[Planner statistics]]). Each row is
  decided by its key, as the walk decides it, so the two cannot answer differently
  (`tests/index_oracle.rs`).

What this bought: a range on a field whose values are all distinct — a name, a size, a
date — has no posting to take whole, and walking it read a key per holder. Beside a
folder, `size > 1000000` read the whole field (16 109 keys on 16 000 records); it now
reads the folder (`tests/kv_cost.rs`, `a_range_on_distinct_values_costs_the_narrow_operand`).

The page's choices — fetch or walk, how far a walk may go — stay with the sort
([[Sorting and postings]]); the anchors of an `osm` path with the forest
([[The forest in the store]]).

!! Measured and not built

Two choices were weighed against measurements and left out; they need not be measured
again unless the repositories grow by an order of magnitude. Both were weighed warm, a
random read priced at ≈ 2 µs; re-measured cold on 9 November 2026 (`L`, 105 000 nodes,
the store evicted before each of three runs — [[Planner statistics]], how the
measurements were taken), where a scattered read costs ≈ 120 µs, both still hold.

* ''An unsorted page under a large subtree.'' `follows_transitive` reads the root's whole descendant bitmap where a page wants a hundred ids. Measured 7 October 2026 (`query.folder` against `query.page` of [[The timed regression suite]], a folder holding the whole repository): +0.08 ms on 21 000 nodes, +0.4 ms on 105 000, about 4 ms extrapolated to a million. The alternative — a per-candidate ancestry check stopping at the page — costs ≈ 100 × depth × 2 µs ≈ 1 ms, so it loses below roughly 300 000 nodes, and its walk cannot be budgeted without the subtree's size, which the store does not keep. ''Cold'': `/dir0` (104 999 files) 47–62 ms against 44–51 ms for the plain page, 251 keys against 247 — the descendant bitmap is a few contiguous keys. The ancestry check would read ≈ 100 × depth scattered keys, ≈ 36 ms at a depth of 3: it loses by more cold than warm.
* ''A two-term `osm` path whose earlier term is common'' (the bench's finder): ≈ 5 ms on 105 000 nodes, nearly all of it each anchor's ancestry (≈ 2 µs a random read). No bitmap formulation was found cheaper on that shape. ''Cold'': `dir1 file12` 577–688 ms, 4 805 keys read resolving it before the index runs; the single term `file12`, which the index serves natively, 421–438 ms for 3 350 keys. So the order-sensitive part costs ≈ 1.4 times the name search itself, and both are the price of ≈ 1 100 scattered nodes (1 111 names hold `file12`, as many `dir1`; 111 paths match). The bitmap formulation — the anchors within the descendants of the nodes naming the earlier term — reads those 1 111 `dir1` nodes and a descendant key each: no fewer keys, as scattered. What would help cold is locality, not another formulation (Open in [[Planner statistics]]).

[[The session scenario]] (a day of GUI use on 50 000 files) showed no query over its
budget: every list, finder and folder step under 1 s on a neutral machine.
